{"name":"Mizu Cyan","hex":"#a7dbed","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cyan","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An additive secondary colour midway between green and blue, evoked by wavelengths between ~490 and ~520 nm."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of the colour cyan."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cyan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Godai (Japanese philosophy)","blocks":[{"text":"Godai (五大; lit. \"five – great, large, physical, form\") are the five elements in Japanese Buddhist thought: earth (chi), water (sui), fire (ka), wind (fu), and void (ku). Its origins are from the Indian Buddhist concept of Mahābhūta, disseminated and influenced by Chinese traditions before being absorbed, influenced, and refined into and by Japanese tradition, culture, and indigenous folk religions."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godai_(Japanese_philosophy)","wikidata":"Q3109675"},{"title":"Cyan","blocks":[{"text":"Cyan is the color between blue and green on the visible spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a predominant wavelength between 490 and 520 nm, between the wavelengths of green and blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Nympheas_71293_3.jpg/330px-Nympheas_71293_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyan","wikidata":"Q180778"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/It-%E7%A9%BA.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/It-%E7%A9%BA.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Void (Aether)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIt-%E7%A9%BA.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/T%C5%8Dky%C5%8D_j%C5%ABnikagetsu%2C_Yanaka_no_y%C5%ABbae_by_Kawase_Hasui.jpg/500px-T%C5%8Dky%C5%8D_